\[
\begin{align*}
\sqrt{3} \cos \theta - \sin \theta &= \frac{1}{3} \\
\cos \theta - \sqrt{3}\sin\theta &= -\frac{4}{9}
\end{align*}
\]
Solving this system, we find the values of $\sin \theta$ and $\cos \theta$. Multiplying the first equation by $\sqrt{3}$ and adding it to the second equation, we get $(3\cos \theta - \sqrt{3}\sin \theta) + (\cos \theta - \sqrt{3}\sin\theta) = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{3} - \frac{4}{9}$. Simplifying, we have $4\cos \theta - 2\sqrt{3}\sin \theta = \frac{3\sqrt{3} - 4}{9}$. Dividing both sides by 2, we obtain $2\cos \theta - \sqrt{3}\sin \theta = \frac{3\sqrt{3} - 4}{18}$. Adding this to the second equation, we get $3\cos \theta = \frac{3\sqrt{3} - 4}{18} - \frac{4}{9}$. Simplifying, we have $3\cos \theta = \frac{3\sqrt{3} - 4 - 8}{18}$. Therefore, $\cos \theta = \frac{3\sqrt{3} - 12}{54}$. Substituting this into the equation $\cos \theta - \sqrt{3}\sin\theta = -\frac{4}{9}$, we get $\frac{3\sqrt{3} - 12}{54} - \sqrt{3}\sin\theta = -\frac{4}{9}$. Solving for $\sin \theta$, we have $\sin \theta = \frac{3\sqrt{3} + 12}{162}$. Finally, we can find $\sqrt{3} \sin \theta + \cos \theta$ by substituting the values of $\sin \theta$ and $\cos \theta$. Therefore, $\sqrt{3} \sin \theta + \cos \theta = \sqrt{3} \left(\frac{3\sqrt{3} + 12}{162}\right) + \frac{3\sqrt{3} - 12}{54}$. Simplifying, we get $\sqrt{3} \sin \theta + \cos \theta = \frac{9 + 4\sqrt{3}}{54} + \frac{3\sqrt{3} - 12}{54}$. Combining the fractions, we have $\sqrt{3} \sin \theta + \cos \theta = \frac{9 + 7\sqrt{3} - 12}{54}$. Simplifying, we obtain $\sqrt{3} \sin \theta + \cos \theta = \frac{7\sqrt{3} - 3}{54}$. </think> <answer> $\
